Practical No: 05

Variables, Control Statements, and Loops

Aim :-

Using shell variables, capturing runtime console arguments, logical paths, and automated loops.

Theory :- 

Dynamic scripting requires storing configuration in variables, 
evaluating conditionals (if-else), and using loop mechanisms (for or while) to handle repetitive control flows.

Requirement:

•Linux
•WSL Terminal

Command explanation :- 

a) Declare and use variable in shell script.

b) Accept user input using read.

$ vi name.sh

code :- 

#!/bin/bash

echo "What is your name:"

read user_name

echo "Hello,$user_name! Nice to meet you."

c) Use conditional statements (if, else)

$ nano ifelse.sh

code :- 

#!/bin/bash

echo "Enter a number:"

read num

if [ $num -gt 0 ]

then

    echo "Positive Number"

else

    echo "Zero or Negative Number"

fi

$ chmod +x ifelse.sh

$ ./ifelse.sh

d) Implement simple loop (for or while)

--- For loop :- 

$ nano forloop.sh

#!/bin/bash

for i in 1 2 3 4 5

do

    echo "Number: $i"

done

$ chmod +x forloop.sh

$ ./forloop.sh

--- While loop :- 

$ nano whileloop.sh

#!/bin/bash

count=1

while [ $count -le 5 ]

do

    echo $count

    count=$((count+1))

done

$ chmod +x whileloop.sh

$ ./whileloop.sh

• Execution :- 

vi name.sh

nano ifelse.sh

chmod +x ifelse.sh

./ifelse.sh

nano forloop.sh

chmod +x forloop.sh

./forloop.sh

nano whileloop.sh

chmod +x whileloop.sh

./whileloop.sh
